<!--
  De jure feudal hierarchy for the DADG feudal titles system (War of the Roses, England 1471;
  Scotland and the Isles are further roots at the end of the file).

  Schema:
    <Feudalism>
      <Kingdom id="..." name="..." [seat="..."] [kingClanId="..."]>
        <Duchy id="..." name="..." [seat="..."] [holderClanId="..."]>
          <County id="..." name="..." [seat="..."] [holderClanId="..."]>
            <Barony id="..." name="..." [seat="..."] [holderClanId="..."]/>
            <Claim clanId="..." [strength="Strong|Weak"]/>  (a claim on the enclosing title)
          </County>
        </Duchy>
        <County .../>  (counties may sit directly under the crown)
      </Kingdom>
    </Feudalism>

  - "id" is required and must be unique across all titles.
  - "seat" is the StringId of the settlement that is the title's seat; it must be unique and
    must match a settlement StringId defined in the game/module data. It is optional for kingdoms.
  - "kingClanId"/"holderClanId" is the StringId of the clan initially holding the title; optional.
  - "heldSince" is the year the holder's house took the title; optional (year 0 when absent).
    It only matters when a clan's top titles share a rank: the oldest one is its primary title
    and so picks its liege. Years are historical where the house really held the dignity or
    seat. Some legacy estate dates are approximate; they are not evidence of allegiance.
    Equal dates fall back to title id. Never tune these dates to distribute vassals.
  - Element names and nesting describe territorial map tiers, not a roll of peerage dignities.
    "dignityRank" (Baron, Earl, Duke) and "dignityName" optionally override the seeded title's
    rank/name without moving any map territory. Baron includes landed
    lordships/chiefs and does not imply every holder was a parliamentary peer.
  - "allegiance" optionally names another title in the same realm, independently of geography.
    Houses follow their highest dignity, then oldest tenure, then title id; they cannot serve
    a house of equal or lower primary rank. Absent an override, territorial nesting remains
    the gameplay fallback, not proof of historical homage. Current political allegiance also
    depends on kingdom membership. Saved territorial reattachments supersede initial links.
    See doc/title-hierarchy-audit-1471.md for sources and unresolved ownership placeholders.
  - "Claim" children are the claims the campaign starts with, on top of the blood claims derived
    from the genealogy: exiled, attainted or dispossessed houses and their lost dignities as of
    spring 1471, each sourced in the comment above it. "strength" defaults to Strong; a Weak
    claim can only be pressed against a woman or a child. A clan may not claim a title it holds.

  Seats mirror DellarteDellaGuerraMap\ModuleData\settlements.xml: every county is seated at one
  of the 28 towns and every barony at a castle. Holders normally mirror the settlement owner,
  with regional/house proxies documented in doc/starting-ownership-audit-1471.md. Starting
  seat owners and title holders are checked together, with only the documented St Andrews
  exception pending the church branch. A proxy is not proof of private ownership.
  The seven English duchy-tier regions are seated
  at castles; their holders' actual dignity may be an earldom. The English crown is seatless: London is held as a county so its de
  jure status survives a change of dynasty on the throne (1471 Readeption: Henry VI of Lancaster).
-->
<Feudalism>
